Troubleshoot Containers and Pods
Objective
Learn how to troubleshoot pods by:
•	inspecting containers
•	running commands inside containers
•	copying files
•	forwarding ports
•	viewing logs and events
These help identify application or configuration issues.
________________________________________
1. Container Troubleshooting Concept
Containers are designed to be:
•	Immutable → not meant to change after creation
•	Ephemeral → temporary
Meaning:
If a container has a problem
→ fix the image
→ rebuild container
→ redeploy
Temporary fixes on a running container are only for debugging.
Correct workflow:
1 Identify problem
2 Fix source code / config
3 Build new container image
4 Redeploy application
________________________________________
2. Important CLI Troubleshooting Commands
Administrators mainly use oc CLI.
Basic inspection
Command	Purpose
oc get	list resources
oc describe	detailed resource information
oc logs	view container logs
oc get events	view cluster events
________________________________________
3. Inspect Resource Details
Use describe to inspect pods.
oc describe pod POD_NAME
Example:
oc describe pod dns-default-lt13h
Information shown:
•	container image
•	node location
•	events
•	restart count
•	IP address
•	volume mounts
This command is very important for troubleshooting.
________________________________________
4. Edit Running Resources
Sometimes configuration must be modified.
Edit resource
oc edit pod POD_NAME
Example:
oc edit pod mongo-app-sw88b
Opens the YAML file in an editor.
Common edits:
•	change environment variables
•	change container image
•	modify ports
________________________________________
5. Patch Resource Fields
Instead of editing the full file, use patch.
Example: change container image.
oc patch pod valid-pod --type='json' \
-p='[{"op": "replace", "path": "/spec/containers/0/image", \
"value":"registry.access.redhat.com/ubi8/httpd-24"}]'
Used when:
•	automation scripts
•	quick configuration updates
________________________________________
6. Copy Files To/From Containers
Sometimes logs or config files must be copied.
Command:
oc cp SOURCE DESTINATION
Copy file from container to host
oc cp apache-app:/var/www/html/index.html /tmp/index.html
Copy file from host to container
oc cp /tmp/index.html apache-app:/var/www/html/
Important rule:
tar must exist inside container
Otherwise the command fails.
________________________________________
7. Synchronize Files (rsync)
For large directories use:
oc rsync SOURCE DESTINATION
Example:
oc rsync apache-app:/var/www/ /tmp/web_files
This synchronizes only changed files.
Requirements:
•	rsync installed locally
•	rsync or tar inside container
________________________________________
8. Remote Shell Access
To connect inside a container:
oc rsh POD_NAME
Example:
oc rsh tomcat-app-jw53r
Output:
sh-4.4#
Now you are inside the container shell.
If pod has multiple containers:
oc rsh -c container_name POD_NAME
________________________________________
9. Execute Commands in Container
Instead of opening shell, run command directly.
Command:
oc exec POD_NAME -- COMMAND
Example:
oc exec mariadb-pod -- ls /
Example output:
bin
boot
dev
etc
home
lib
________________________________________
Interactive execution
Often used with:
-it
Example:
oc exec -it mariadb-pod -- bash
Meaning:
•	-i interactive
•	-t terminal
________________________________________
10. View Container Logs
Logs are the most common troubleshooting tool.
Command:
oc logs POD_NAME
Example:
oc logs php-app-5d9b84b588-kzfxd
Logs show:
•	errors
•	application startup
•	crashes
•	database issues
________________________________________
Logs for specific container
If multiple containers exist:
oc logs POD_NAME -c CONTAINER_NAME
________________________________________
11. View Cluster Events
Kubernetes records cluster events.
Command:
oc get events
Example output:
Pulled container image
Created container
Started container
Events show:
•	image pull errors
•	scheduling failures
•	network problems
•	pod restarts
________________________________________
12. Port Forwarding
Sometimes pods do not expose ports externally.
Use port forwarding to access them locally.
Command:
oc port-forward POD LOCAL_PORT:CONTAINER_PORT
Example:
oc port-forward nginx-pod 8080:80
Result:
localhost:8080 → pod:80
Now open browser:
http://localhost:8080
Very useful for debugging.
________________________________________
13. Troubleshooting from Inside Cluster
Sometimes debugging requires tools that are not inside the container.
Solution:
Create a toolbox container.
Example tools:
•	mysql client
•	curl
•	ping
•	netcat
•	dig
•	tcpdump
Example use case:
Test if one pod can connect to another pod
Example:
mysql -h mysql-server-pod
This helps test network and service connectivity.
________________________________________
14. Why Not Install Tools in Production Containers
Adding debugging tools increases:
•	container size
•	security risk
•	maintenance overhead
Better approach:
Use a temporary toolbox container
________________________________________
15. Typical Troubleshooting Workflow
When a pod fails:
Step 1
oc get pods
Check status.
________________________________________
Step 2
oc describe pod POD_NAME
Check events.
________________________________________
Step 3
oc logs POD_NAME
Find application errors.
________________________________________
Step 4
oc exec -it POD_NAME -- bash
Inspect inside container.
________________________________________
Step 5
oc port-forward POD 8080:80
Test service locally.
________________________________________
Step 6
oc cp
oc rsync
Copy configuration files if needed.
________________________________________
16. Most Important Commands (Exam Focus)
These commands are used very frequently in OpenShift troubleshooting.
Command	Purpose
oc get pods	list pods
oc describe pod	inspect pod
oc logs	view container logs
oc exec	run command inside container
oc rsh	open container shell
oc port-forward	access pod port
oc cp	copy files
oc rsync	sync directories
oc edit	edit resource
oc patch	update resource fields
________________________________________
One-Line Summary
Troubleshooting containers = inspect pods, view logs, run commands inside containers, copy files, and test connectivity.

